## Task
Fill the numbered holes «n» in the sketch of export const find. Everything else in the sketch is fixed and is checked to be unchanged (OARC-E099).

## Sketch (fixed; FindFn = (haystack: number[], needle: number): number)
(haystack, needle) => {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = haystack.length - 1;
  let found = -1;
  while (lo <= hi && found === -1) {
    const mid = «1»;
    if (haystack[mid] === needle) {
      found = mid;
    } else {
      «2»
    }
  }
  «3»
  return found;
}

## Holes
«1» one expression of type number: index of the middle element of the part haystack[lo..hi] not yet eliminated
«2» statements (no return, break or continue out of the hole; don't declare a name the sketch uses later): eliminate the middle element and the half that cannot contain needle, by moving lo or hi
«3» statements (no return, break or continue out of the hole; don't declare a name the sketch uses later): every element has been eliminated when found === -1: handle needle not being in the list

## Imports already in the file
import * as arc from "open-arc";

## Types (verbatim)
export type FindFn = (haystack: number[], needle: number) => number;

## Intent
Exercise "binary-search": implement `find`. Your task is to implement a binary search algorithm. A binary search algorithm finds an item in a list by repeatedly splitting it in half, only keeping the half which contains the item we're looking for. It allows us to quickly narrow down the possible locations of our item until we find it, or until we've eliminated all possible locations. ~~~~exercism/caution Binary search only works when a list has been sorted. ~~~~ The algorithm looks like this: - Find the middle element of a _sorted_ list and compare it with the item we're looking for. - If the middle element is our item, then we're done! - If the middle element is greater than our item, we can eliminate that element and all the elements **after** it. - If the middle element is less than our item, we can eliminate that element and all the elements **before** it. - If every element of the list has been eliminated then the item is not in the list. - Otherwise, repeat the process on the part of the list that has not been eliminated. Here's an example: Let's say we're looking for the number 23 in the following sorted list: `[4, 8, 12, 16, 23, 28, 32]`. - We start by comparing 23 with the middle element, 16. - Since 23 is greater than 16, we can eliminate the left half of the list, leaving us with `[23, 28, 32]`. - We then compare 23 with the new middle element, 28. - Since 23 is less than 28, we can eliminate the right half of the list: `[23]`. - We've found our item.

## Calls allowed in fills
You may call: nothing (what the sketch itself calls). Any other call on an import or a port is rejected (OARC-E098); calls on locals and on functions a fill defines are fine.

